{
  "sticker": "ammonite",
  "called": "the ammonite",
  "skeleton": "tiny ammonite afloat, seen from the side facing left: root under it; body (pivot at the middle of the shell) with belly squash carrying the coiled shell (drawn empty, its opening a dark hollow) and, in front of everything, the shell's front lip arching over the opening; head (the soft orange body peeking out of the opening, pivot inside the opening) with face (both eyes, the far one smaller, brows and mouth); under the head a back fringe of short tentacles (shaded) and five front tentacles, each on its own bone at its root (t1 curling up at the front, t2–t5 hanging); the head slips into the opening behind the lip to hide.",
  "sheets": [
    {
      "id": "parts",
      "size": "1536x1024",
      "prompt": "Image 1 is a sticker of a tiny, cute ammonite: a round coiled spiral shell with soft ridges in cream, coral and rust stripes, its soft orange spotted body peeking out of the shell's opening on the left with a fringe of short curly tentacles underneath, facing left. Draw this exact ammonite as a cut-out paper puppet: its separate parts laid out on one sheet, the same drawing style, colours, texture, line and proportions as in image 1, every part at the size it has in image 1.\n\nDraw each part on its own, spread out over the sheet with wide empty gaps between them, no part touching or overlapping another:\n1. the shell, empty: the whole round coiled spiral shell with its soft ridges and its cream, coral and rust stripes, exactly as in image 1, with nothing in it: no body, no face, no tentacles; its big opening on the left a deep, dark brown hollow, drawn whole.\n2. the shell's front lip alone: only the curved, ridged front rim of the shell that arches over the top of the opening in image 1, a narrow curved band in the same stripes, both of its ends soft with NO outline across them.\n3. the soft body alone: the round orange spotted body that peeks out of the opening in image 1, with its rosy cheek, turned as in image 1, with a completely blank face: no eyes, no eyebrows, no mouth; without any tentacles; its right side (normally inside the shell) and its bottom (where the tentacles join) a soft edge with NO outline, the dark outline only round its top and front.\n4. the back fringe: a row of five short, curly orange tentacles side by side with pale spots, hanging down and curling at their tips, joined along the top by a soft orange band with NO outline across its top.\n5. one tentacle curling up: the orange tentacle with pale spots at the far left of image 1, curling up and outwards at its tip, drawn whole from its rounded root to its curl; its root a soft rounded end with NO outline across it.\n6. one tentacle hanging down, its tip curling round to the left, orange with pale spots, drawn whole from its rounded root to its curl; its root a soft rounded end with NO outline across it.\n7. one tentacle hanging down, its tip curling round to the right, orange with pale spots, drawn whole from its rounded root to its curl; its root a soft rounded end with NO outline across it.\n\nEvery part is drawn whole, including what is normally hidden behind another part. Nothing sharp. No water, no bubbles, no text, no labels, no numbers, no outline around the parts beyond the drawing's own line, no shadows. Fully transparent background."
    },
    {
      "id": "face",
      "size": "1536x1024",
      "references": [
        "parts"
      ],
      "prompt": "Image 1 is a sticker of a tiny, cute ammonite; image 2 shows the same ammonite cut into puppet parts, with a blank face. Draw the ammonite's face pieces as separate stickers on one sheet: the same drawing style, colours and line as in image 1, every piece at the size it has on the ammonite in image 1, so it fits the blank face in image 2. Its face is turned a little to the left: its far eye (on the left of image 1) is a little smaller than its near eye (on the right of image 1). In image 1 both eyes look to the left: each big dark-brown iris sits towards the left of its eye, the white on its right.\n\nDraw each piece on its own, upright, in four tidy rows with wide empty gaps between them, nothing touching:\nRow 1, the far eye (the smaller eye, on the LEFT of image 1), five times:\n1. as it is in image 1: a big round dark-brown iris with a white sparkle, looking to the left.\n2. wide open and surprised: bigger and rounder, a warm-brown iris with a white sparkle, looking the same way.\n3. closed in a happy smile: an upward-curving arc, like an upside-down U.\n4. sad: open and round, a soft warm-brown iris with a white sparkle, looking a little down and to the left; no lid slanting across it.\n5. gently closed in sleep: a soft downward-curving line as wide as the open eye, with a few short lashes.\nRow 2, the near eye (the bigger eye, on the RIGHT of image 1), the same five in the same order; open, it looks the same way as row 1, to the left, the iris towards the left of the eye.\nRow 3: 11. the far eyebrow (over the left eye of image 1) as it is in image 1, a short thin dark-brown arch. 12. the same far eyebrow worried: tilted up at its inner (right) end. 13. the near eyebrow (over the right eye of image 1) as it is in image 1, a short thin dark-brown arch. 14. the same near eyebrow worried: tilted up at its inner (left) end.\nRow 4, small mouths, none with any teeth: 15. the mouth as it is in image 1: a small, thin, closed smiling line. 16. the mouth open in a happy laugh, rounded, the inside pink with a little pink tongue. 17. the mouth puckered into a small round 'o', the inside pink, as for blowing bubbles. 18. the mouth in a small sad frown. 19. the mouth open in a round 'O', as for a big yawn, the inside pink.\n\nNo text, no labels, no numbers, no shadows, no face or head around the pieces. Fully transparent background."
    }
  ],
